cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use FALCONAge, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "FALCONAge: Multiomic Biological Age and Aging Clock Scoring in Python and R"
abstract: >
  Scores DNA methylation and clinical chemistry data against a catalogue of 161
  published aging clocks and provides the downstream statistics the field
  expects: age acceleration in its three conventions, survival and association
  models, technical and biological reliability, and the AA1/AA2 case-control
  benchmark. Every clock architecture is written from its published description
  rather than ported from another package, and each registry entry declares the
  scale its output lives on, which governs the operations the package will
  permit -- subtracting a chronological age from a pace-of-aging clock is
  refused rather than computed. Coefficients are treated separately from
  architectures because they are fitted data with their own licences: those that
  may be redistributed ship with the package, and the twenty-eight that may not
  are implemented as tested scaffolds the user supplies numbers for. The R
  interface delegates all arithmetic to the Python core through reticulate, so
  a result obtained in one language is the same bits as a result obtained in the
  other rather than two implementations that agree approximately.
authors:
  # CFF requires the ORCID as a full URL, not the bare digits. DESCRIPTION holds
  # the same identifier in the form R expects, which is digits only.
  # `affiliation:` is still unset; add it here if you want it in the citation.
  - given-names: "Bhagesh"
    family-names: "Hunakunti"
    email: "bhunakun@uni-bonn.de"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5957-8005"
version: 1.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-10"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/bhagesh-h/FALCONAge"
url: "https://bhagesh-h.github.io/FALCONAge/"
keywords:
  - biological age
  - aging clock
  - epigenetic clock
  - DNA methylation
  - age acceleration
  - reproducible research
  - Python
  - R
# The 161 clocks FALCONAge implements are due to their original authors, and a
# result computed with one of them should cite that paper as well as this
# package. They are not duplicated here because a CFF `references:` block wants
# a complete author list per entry, and 161 incomplete ones in a
# machine-readable file are worse than none. The registry carries the citation
# and DOI for every clock instead, so the reference for whichever clocks a
# particular analysis used can be produced from the analysis itself:
#
#   Python:  falconage.registry.load().get("grimage2").cite("bibtex")
#   R:       cite_clock("grimage2", "bibtex")
#   CLI:     falconage clocks cite grimage2 --style bibtex
#
# inst/CITATION carries the same entry as this file for `citation("FALCONAge")`.
